<div class="article-body">

<p>I grew cilantro three times and all three shot up a skinny stalk with little white flowers before I got a single taco's worth of leaves. Then the leaves turned soapy and bitter. That is bolting, and it is the single most common way indoor cilantro dies as a crop.</p>

<p>Here is the short version before I show you the steps: <strong>most beginners bolt cilantro by giving it too much light, not too little.</strong> Cut it to 12 hours and the plant stays in leaf mode for weeks. That one change, plus the right seed, turned my cilantro from a 2-week disappointment into a 2-month supply.</p>

<p>Disclosure up front: I am not a scientist. I am someone who kept buying cilantro seeds and watching them turn into flower stalks before figuring out the one number that mattered.</p>

<h2 id="why">1. Why Your Cilantro Bolts</h2>

<p>Bolting is a survival response. Cilantro is a cool-season annual that wants to make seed before summer heat kills it. When it reads long days, warm air, or dry roots, it decides "time to reproduce" and throws up a flower stalk. Once that stalk appears, the leaves taste soapy and the plant is basically done for leaves.</p>

<p>Three triggers cause nearly every case of premature bolting indoors:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Heat above 75°F (24°C).</strong> Warm water and warm air both tell cilantro it is late spring. This is the #1 trigger.</li>
  <li><strong>Long days.</strong> More than about 14 hours of light reads as summer. This is the one most people never consider, and the one that matters most under LED grow lights.</li>
  <li><strong>Dry roots.</strong> Even a brief dry spell pushes cilantro toward bolting. Moisture stress is a reproduction signal.</li>
</ul>

<p>Here is the part that surprised me: the plant does not bolt because you did something wrong. It bolts because you built it a summer. Your job is to build it a spring, on purpose.</p>

<h2 id="trick">2. The 12-Hour Fix Nobody Tells You About</h2>

<p>Every generic herb setup runs the light 14 to 16 hours a day. That works for basil and mint. It is actively bad for cilantro, because cilantro is a long-day plant.</p>

<p>Long-day means longer daylight tells it to flower. So when I ran my LED for 16 hours to make my basil happy, I was telling my cilantro it was July. It bolted every single time.</p>

<p>Here is the key insight: give cilantro <strong>12 hours on, 12 hours off</strong>. Not 14, not 16. Twelve. This fakes autumn, keeps it in leaf production, and buys you several extra weeks of harvestable leaves. It is the cheapest change you can make because it takes one timer plug and zero new gear.</p>

<p>I picked up a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=outlet+timer+plug+for+grow+lights&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">timer plug for grow lights</a> for about $10 and set it to 12 hours. That single change did more than every nutrient trick I had tried combined.</p>

<h2 id="seeds">3. The Seed Trick That Buys You 3 Extra Weeks</h2>

<p>The other change that matters most: buy slow-bolt varieties. Generic cilantro from a big packet bolts in 3 to 4 weeks. A variety bred to resist bolting, like <strong>Calypso, Cruiser, or Santo</strong>, reliably gives you 6 to 8 weeks of leaves under the same growing conditions.</p>

<p>That is nearly double the harvest from the exact same effort. If you are growing from grocery-store seeds or a generic herb packet, you are fighting the fastest-bolting genes available. That is like signing up for a race you did not know you were in.</p>

<p>Pay attention to this number: switching to slow-bolt seeds took my usable harvest from about 3 weeks to nearly 7. Same light, same water, same spot. The only variable was the seed in the ground. Look for packets labeled <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=slow+bolt+cilantro+seeds+Calypso&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">slow bolt cilantro seeds</a> and grab a Calypso.</p>

<h2 id="hydro">4. Why Hydroponics Beats Soil For This Crop</h2>

<p>Cilantro is the one herb where I recommend hydroponics over soil for beginners, because the biggest bolting trigger in soil is dry roots, and hydroponics removes that entirely.</p>

<p>In soil, you forget to water on a hot day and the roots dry out for a few hours. That tiny stress nudges cilantro toward bolting. In a hydroponic setup, the roots sit in water around the clock. No dry cycle, no moisture stress, ever. The same variety that bolts in 3 to 4 weeks in a pot gives you 5 to 7 weeks in water.</p>

<p>I grow mine Kratky style in a mason jar with a net cup, no pump, no electricity. If you have not tried it, the <a href="/posts/kratky-method-for-beginners">Kratky method for beginners</a> guide walks through the whole setup I use.</p>

<p>Keep the water cool and the pH in the right range. Cilantro likes pH 6.0 to 6.5, which is the upper end of what most herbs want. A basic <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+nutrient+test+kit+pH&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">hydroponic pH test kit</a> runs about $12 and sorts it out. If you aim it at the nutrient mixes, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+nutrient+kit+herbs&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">hydroponic nutrient kit for herbs</a> is all you need, around $20.</p>

<h2 id="harvest">5. How To Harvest Without Killing The Plant</h2>

<p>Most people harvest cilantro wrong and accidentally speed up bolting. Here is the rule: <strong>cut outer stems, never the middle</strong>. The center crown is where new leaves come from. If you chop the middle, the plant cannot regrow and it races to flower instead.</p>

<p>Start harvesting when the plant is about 4 to 6 inches tall. Take the oldest outer stems at the base, and never remove more than one third of the plant at a time. Repeat every 7 to 10 days. This constant light cutting keeps it in leaf mode, exactly like pinching basil keeps it bushy.</p>

<p>Then the real trick for an endless supply: <strong>succession plant.</strong> Cilantro bolts eventually no matter what. So do not bet everything on one plant. Start a new jar or pot every 2 to 3 weeks. By the time the first one bolts and goes bitter, the second is at peak, and the third is coming up. That is how I went from "taco night ruined" to free cilantro all season.</p>

<h2 id="honest">6. The Honest Truth About Cilantro</h2>

<p>Here is the honest truth: you cannot stop cilantro from bolting forever. It is a short-lived annual programmed to reproduce fast. No trick, no seed, no light schedule makes it a perpetual plant like mint. Even under perfect conditions, it will flower eventually.</p>

<p>That is not a failure. It is how the plant is built. The goal is not to stop bolting. The goal is to delay it long enough that you get weeks of leaves, and to have the next plant ready when this one gives up. Once you stop fighting the biology and start planning around it, cilantro becomes one of the easiest herbs on your shelf.</p>

<p>One honest caveat: some people taste cilantro as soapy no matter what, because of a gene. If that is you, even perfect cilantro will taste like dish soap and no growing method fixes it. That is biology too, just yours.</p>

<p>Want more herbs that survive beginner mistakes? The <a href="/posts/best-herbs-for-hydroponics-beginners">best herbs for hydroponics beginners</a> list has the easy wins, and if you are choosing a grow light for that shelf, compare real specs on the <a href="/grow-lights">Grow Light DB</a>.</p>

<p>👉 <a href="/posts/how-to-grow-basil-indoors">Basil is the easy sibling: here is my full guide →</a></p>

<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> If your cilantro keeps bolting, stop giving it summer. Run the light 12 hours, buy slow-bolt Calypso seeds, grow it in water so the roots never dry, and plant a fresh one every 3 weeks. My timer plug was $10 when I got it and the Calypso seeds ran about $6 for 600. If you can keep the reservoir under 75°F and the days short, cilantro goes from your hardest herb to one you can stop buying at the store. Let my three flower stalks take the hit so yours do not have to.</p>

